From: slwyts <slwyts@foxmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fastrpc: freeze userspace invocation waiters in place

The sensor daemon can wait indefinitely for an inbound DSP listener call.
Its interruptible completion wait is woken by the process freezer and
returns -ERESTARTSYS. The interrupted context is retained, but retrying
the ioctl allocates and sends another context rather than resuming it.
This can break the listener protocol across suspend.

Use the existing completion API with TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE | TASK_FREEZABLE.
The freezer can stop and resume the waiter without resending the RPC.
Ordinary signal interruption remains supported, and the bounded kernel
invocation path is unchanged. This candidate needs suspend/resume testing
with the real sensor daemon; it does not implement general ioctl restart.
---
diff --git a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
index 3819dc613ec..4695d79e122 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
@@ -1283,7 +1283,11 @@ static int fastrpc_internal_invoke(struct fastrpc_user *fl,  u32 kernel,
 		if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&ctx->work, 10 * HZ))
 			err = -ETIMEDOUT;
 	} else {
-		err = wait_for_completion_interruptible(&ctx->work);
+		/* Freeze this waiter in place during system sleep, retaining the
+		 * in-flight DSP context instead of restarting and resending it.
+		 * Real signals remain interruptible. */
+		err = wait_for_completion_state(&ctx->work,
+						TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE | TASK_FREEZABLE);
 	}
 
 	if (err)
